-- title:
--   Modular polynomial as minimal polynomial of j(q^N) over L(j(q))
-- statement:
--   Let $L$ be a field equipped with a $\mathbb{Q}$-algebra structure, let $N$ be a natural number with $N \neq 0$, and let `data` be an element of `ModularPolynomialData N`, that is: a polynomial $\Phi \in (\mathbb{Z}[X])[Y]$ together with proofs that $\Phi$ is monic, that its degree in $Y$ equals $\psi(N) = \sum_{d \mid N,\ d \text{ squarefree}} N/d$ (`dedekindPsi N`), and that $\Phi$ vanishes when its coefficients in $\mathbb{Z}[X]$ are evaluated by $X \mapsto jq$ and $Y \mapsto jqN\,N$ inside the Laurent series field over $\mathbb{Q}$. Work in `LaurentSeries L`, with $j_L :=$ `jqModC L` the Laurent series $q^{-1}$ times the image under $\mathbb{Z} \to L$ of the power series `jNum` $= E_4^3 \cdot$ `dedekindEtaUnitInv`, and $j_{L,N} :=$ `jqNModC L N`, its image under the ring homomorphism `qExpand L N` obtained by re-indexing exponents along multiplication by $N$ (substitution $q \mapsto q^N$). The assertion is that the minimal polynomial of $j_{L,N}$ over the intermediate field $L(j_L) =$ `IntermediateField.adjoin L {jqModC L}` is exactly the image of $\Phi$ under the coefficientwise map $\mathbb{Z}[X] \to L(j_L)$ sending integers to their images and $X$ to $j_L$; in words, $\Phi(j_L, Y)$ is the minimal polynomial of $j_{L,N}$ over $L(j_L)$.
--
--   This is the classical statement that the level-$N$ modular polynomial $\Phi_N(X,Y)$, specialised at $X = j$, is irreducible of degree $\psi(N)$ over the field generated by $j$, here in the formal $q$-expansion model of the function field of $X_0(N)$ and over an arbitrary field of characteristic zero. The proof combines the vanishing, monicity and degree clauses of the modular-polynomial data with the relative-degree computation [`ModularCurve.relfinrank_full_eq_dedekindPsi`](thm.html#ModularCurve.relfinrank_full_eq_dedekindPsi), its base change to $L$, the generation statement [`ModularCurve.functionFieldGeneration`](thm.html#ModularCurve.functionFieldGeneration), and transcendence of $j_L$ over $L$; it is used downstream in the integrality and localisation arguments at the nodes of the modular curve.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_minpoly_jqNModC_eq.lean

import Definitions.Def_ModularCurve_X0
import Definitions.Def_ModularCurve_JqCoeff
import Mathlib.FieldTheory.Minpoly.Field

set_option maxHeartbeats 4000000
set_option synthInstance.maxHeartbeats 400000
set_option backward.isDefEq.respectTransparency.types false

open ModularCurve

theorem ModularCurve.minpoly_jqNModC_eq (L : Type*) [Field L] [Algebra ℚ L] (N : ℕ) [NeZero N]
    (data : ModularPolynomialData N) :
    minpoly (↥(IntermediateField.adjoin L ({jqModC L} : Set (LaurentSeries L)))) (jqNModC L N) =
      data.Φ.map (Polynomial.eval₂RingHom
        (Int.castRingHom (↥(IntermediateField.adjoin L ({jqModC L} : Set (LaurentSeries L)))))
        ⟨jqModC L, IntermediateField.mem_adjoin_simple_self L (jqModC L)⟩) := by sorry
